Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi,
 Finn Lindgren showed me this method to do a sort of local significance testing using INLA, which I take to be looking at where local values have a > 95% probability of differing from global values. I've been using it in spatial models to circumscribe areas where local values differ from average values.

mean1 <- model_2$summary.fitted.values[inla.stack.index(stk.full, "prediction")$data,"mean"]
sd1 <- model_2$summary.fitted.values[inla.stack.index(stk.full, "prediction")$data,"sd"]
m1 <- mean(mean2)
p1 <- 2*pmin(pnorm(m2, mean2, sd2), 1-pnorm(m2, mean2, sd2))

I use this not just on the model fit, but on the prediction (I predict to a dense grid covering the spatial study area). That way I can output a prediction surface along with this "significance" contour as in the below image.

Is there a way to do something similar using a brms model output (or better yet a prediction)? Thanks!
Paul

