Yes, you can do the same thing with "estimate" and "est.error" in brms.

However, this assumes your predictions to be normally distributed, which may not necessarily be the case.

Better go fo

fv <- fitted(model, summary = FALSE)
m <- mean(fv)
prob_greater <- colMeans(fv > m)
prob_smaller <.- 1 - prob_greater

Please also not that in brms we have to distinquish between "fitted" and "predict". The former predicts the mean of the response distribution, the latter predicts new response values.
See ?fitted.brmsfit and ?predict.brmsfit for more details.

Best,
Paul

